                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1602-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>ADK Berlin 47 Au</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Berlin 47 Au is a Cardioid FET condenser with a large-diaphragm capsule and transformer-coupled output. It was introduced around 2009 as part of ADK&#8217;s &#8220;Custom Shop&#8221; series; it was replaced in 2013 with the release of the T-Fet series of multipattern mics.</p><p><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/capsules/#K47"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/barecapsules/front/sm/adk-gk47.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="ADK/3-Zigma GK47 capsule" width="150" height="150" /></a>The 47 Au uses the GK47 capsule from ADK&#8217;s 3-Zigma product line; this is a clone of the Neumann K47 capsule, voiced to sound like current production K47 capsules. </p><p>The circuit is made with high-quality parts, including Wima capacitors, a premium JFET, and an Oxford/OEP output transformer.</p><p>Two switches located below the headbasket allow for multiple configuration options:</p><p>On the rear of the mic, the first of two 3-position switch allows selection of two high-pass filters: -6dB/octave at either 100Hz or 160Hz.</p><p>A second 3-way switch controls the input pad: 0dB, -8dB, or -16dB.</p><p>The body is engraved with the &#8220;ADK Custom Shop&#8221; logo and the model name of the mic.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/ADK/Berlin-47-Au">ADK Berlin-47-Au</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
